<p>Early in the pandemic, hospitals needed to know which COVID-19 patients were at highest risk of poor outcomes. Many prediction models had been published, but external validation was rare and usually done on data from other Wuhan hospitals.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We derived models in 775 adult inpatients from two hospitals in Wuhan, using demographics, comorbidities, symptoms and laboratory tests. We then tested the best models on 226 inpatients at Kingâ€™s College Hospital in London.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>In the Wuhan data, models using age, sex and routine blood tests (neutrophil, lymphocyte and platelet counts, C-reactive protein and creatinine) discriminated very well, with a c-index of 0.91 for death. In London, where far more patients died (34.1% against 4.3%), discrimination was still good (0.74) but the predicted risk groups were off: observed death rates were much higher than predicted.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>A model can look excellent where it was built and still disappoint elsewhere. We called for larger derivation datasets and rapid external validation of every proposed model before routine clinical use.</p>
